[{:seon.task/name "a-resource-name-stays-in-its-root"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by jolt/similar-finds-helpers-by-embedding (2026-10-23): in a worktree nested under the main tree (next/.worktrees/NAME), ``seon.config-test/an-unknown-key-refuses-boot-naming-it`` (deleted 2026-11-16 with the closed map) and `an-invalid-value-refuses-boot-naming-each-key` read the MAIN tree's resources/seon/config/default.edn, so a branch that adds config keys is red there and one that removes keys could be falsely green. Cause: Jolt's resource lookup over jolt.loader's roots, `resource-candidate` (reference-code/jolt/host/chez/java/io.ss, called from `resolve-resource`) builds root + \"/\" + name with no check that the normalized path stays under root, and aero's adaptive resolver tries ``clojure.java.io/resource`` before the including file's directory, so test/seon/config/unknown-key.edn's #include \"../../../resources/seon/config/default.edn\" climbs from the worktree's src/ root out to next/resources/. The JVM's resource lookup answers nil for a name that leaves its root. Reproduction, in a nested worktree: HOME=$PWD/target/home JOLT_PWD=$PWD target/jolt/jolt -A:test -e '(println (str (clojure.java.io/resource \"../../../resources/seon/config/default.edn\")))' prints a file: URL into the main tree; the JVM prints nil. Correct, in our Jolt fork on a jolt/<topic> branch: `resource-candidate` answers no candidate for a name whose normalized path leaves its root. Notes: side-session-notes config-include-leak.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.loader-test/clojure-java-io-resource-resources-seon-config-default}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-cache-bound-is-tested-at-the-targets-size-before-release"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes. What exists: Jolt keeps its compiled-code cache under a byte budget, least recently used first (``JOLT_CACHE_MB``, Jolt `b12a223d`; docs/source.md#shared-build-caches), and bin/env sets it to 32768 so that nothing is evicted while we develop (owner, 2026-11-11: \"Is this going to slow down development? Will we be able to lower this later to test before we release it?\"). Measured 2026-11-11 on the shared cache: one worktree's builds and tests on a new runtime, 354 MB in 890 artifacts; every session's worktrees on one runtime over a day and a half, 3.2 GB in 3,336; the whole cache 4.6 GB in four generations. So 4096 holds one checkout's working set about ten times over and is the candidate for the 16 GB target. Correct, before a release: the bound is set from the working set of one checkout's full build and prove measured on a clean cache, and a run at that bound shows no artifact of the running tree evicted (no recompile of a namespace the same process tree compiled) and the cache directory at or under the bound after it. Proof when built: a check step that builds and proves one tree with ``JOLT_CACHE_MB`` at the release number on an empty cache directory of its own, and counts recompiles of already-compiled namespaces (0) and the directory's bytes (at most the bound)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.loader-test/the-cache-bound-is-tested-at-the-targets-size-before-release}}

 {:seon.task/name "a-forms-eval-reuses-its-compiled-code-by-digest", :seon.task/plan "Measured (deps, 2026-11-14, 100 seon.program forms): a form's eval costs 1.17M ticks and 14 MB with frame tracing (0.83M and 9 MB without; tracing is ~30%), Chez's compile of the emitted Scheme two thirds of it; the emitted text is not a key as emitted (a process-wide label counter numbers each form's locals) but is once its labels are renumbered per form. Agreed with runtime and root, 2026-11-14: not built now. Runtime's lever 3 (an agent behind main re-forks from the warm fork server holding main compiled and loads only its own diff) removes a merge's 1,028 main-form loads (2.59B ticks, 30 GB measured by runtime); after it the cache would save one of the two compiles a landed form gets (root and the fork server, ~0.78M ticks a form) and a branch's own diff per re-fork (context's branch: 8 forms, ~16 reloads in its life, ~12M ticks), well under its ~1.5 days. Re-measure after lever 3 lands: the compiles per landed form across processes; build only if they show a real cost. Plan: plans/deps-eval-by-digest.md. docs/source.md#a-merge-loads-in-place.", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-merge-of-unchanged-forms-compiles-none}}
 {:seon.task/name "compiled-code-costs-what-its-source-needs"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps. Asked by tests2 (2026-11-16, agent b34f395149ab6b39, one eval per test namespace with a collection between): the test registry holds 305 MB, each test namespace 100-350x its source (render-test 433 KB source -> 47 MB, agent-test 350 KB -> 28 MB, reconcile-test 229 KB -> 24 MB, config-test and vector-test 43 KB -> 10-15 MB); fixtures add <1 MB each; the heap census beside the code: rp-info 26 MB, reloc-table 11 MB, source objects 8 MB, procedures 22 MB. PRIOR WORK, read: Jolt's build table (reference-code/jolt/host/chez/build.ss:3292-3299) compiles a release app half with generate-inspector-information and generate-procedure-source-information ON (the optimized row OFF); the runtime half is always OFF (bld-runtime-chez-params, build.ss:3317), measured there as 57% of a hello-world binary's bytes (burinc/jolt#3: 27.25 MB -> 11.68 MB, 225 -> 132 MB resident). Forms compiled at run time take the process's current parameters (compile-eval.ss:597 jolt-ce-compile-tag; loader.ss sa-compile-file with #f, Chez's defaults: inspector ON). MEASURED BY DEPS (2026-11-16, probes built with our Jolt 054395e7 and with upstream jolt-lang/jolt 9cf28bdb, vmmap PRV on the running pid, Chez (bytes-allocated (quote static)) after a full collection): a minimal program is 52.0 MB private on ours and 51.4 MB on upstream (our 426 Jolt commits: +0.6 MB), its static image 72 MB, Jolt's compiler included (27 namespaces). Requiring only datahike.api: 176 namespaces, 183 MB static, 229 MB private; JOLT_TRACE=0 at build 168 static / 198 private; JOLT_TRACE=0 and --opt 151 static / 179 private. So upstream's tracing baked into built binaries by default (host/chez/build.ss:2950, measured upstream in run time only) and Chez's inspector information (release mode) cost 50 MB of 229. Datahike's stack is <=3.05 MB of source -> 30.4 MB of emitted Scheme (10x) -> 19.8 MB of compressed objects -> ~111 MB of image in memory. One 4-line fn (datahike.query.estimate/cmp-entity-only, 134 chars) emits 2,064 chars: a jolt-site-call thunk per call, jolt-site! position records, its var metadata map rebuilt at load, jolt-register-source! and callsite tables. Seon full: contracts armed at definition +101 MB at start (443.5 vs 544.5 private, :seon.config.contract/instrument :off); --opt: in use after a full collection 343 vs 386 MB. Still unexplained: what the remaining ~80 MB of Datahike image and ~75 MB of Seon image are, by object kind (next: Chez compute-composition of the static generation). HYPOTHESIS, untested: the run-time compiles of test namespaces (and the release app half) carry inspector and source information. TEST, at a fresh agent: read (optimize-level) (debug-level) (generate-inspector-information) (generate-procedure-source-information) as they stand; then one test namespace compiled with them on against off, the form cache bypassed, retained bytes after a full collection each. If it holds, the trade (smaller code against backtraces naming less) is the owner's, with both numbers."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-body-change-recompiles-only-what-inlined-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps. Asked by tests2 and the owner (2026-11-16): every bin/build pays for the whole program whatever moved. MEASURED (deps, 2026-11-16, JOLT_BUILD_PROFILE=1, a body-only edit of seon.agent/with-slot, 421 units with the tests compiled in): 120 s, of it load app from source 30.2 s, whole-program inference 62.3 s (fixpoint 16.1 s, analyze 3.8 s), emit app namespaces 22.3 s (run-passes 10.0 s, emit-top 9.9 s), write flat.ss 2.3 s, and compile app units 1.8 s for the 11 of 421 whose emitted Scheme changed. The Chez step is already exact (bld-unit-key is the emitted text, build.ss:4007); the waste is everything before it, rerun whole. Lower bound for this edit: load, infer and emit the changed namespace and those whose inferred facts or spliced bodies it moves, well under 1 s; today is >100x that. READ (not yet measured): Jolt's AOT key already narrows an inert dependency to its var-name surface (loader.ss aot-ct-relevant?, aot-ns-surface), but aot-narrow? is off under jolt build, since direct linking splices inline-eligible bodies across namespaces (host-contract.ss:970-1000). MEASURED, the same edit with JOLT_NO_WP_INFER=1 (Jolt's documented escape: emit falls back to per-namespace inference, build.ss:2966): 45 s, load app from source 1.6 s, whole-program inference 1.3 s, emit 24.6 s, compile app units 13.8 s (367/421 recompiled, since code emitted without the inference differs; a repeat would hit the cache, ~31 s expected). Not like for like yet: the load's 30.2 -> 1.6 s may be a warmer cache on the second run. OWED before any switch: the runtime cost of dropping inference, the ticks of one fixed workload (seon.main-test's laws at an agent, 262M ticks on the inferred binary, result/adb8b512) on both binaries. Then emit (22-25 s whole every build) is the next cost. DESIGN to settle first, at the profile: (1) the load from source reuses a namespace's previous analysis when its inputs are unchanged (its text, its requires' surfaces, the bodies it spliced); (2) whole-program inference is re-solved from the changed namespaces' facts outward, its fixpoint seeded from the last build's solution; (3) emit runs only for namespaces whose analysis or inferred facts changed. Each step measured with the same edit before and after. Proof, red first: a two-namespace build where b requires a; an edit to a's non-inlined defn body re-emits a only; an edit to an inlined one re-emits both."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}]
